Subject: [PATCH 0/2] hurd: Fix two races exposed by SMP

Hello,

These two Hurd fixes come out of running Debian GNU/Hurd amd64 with
gnumach's processors all in the default processor set (2 vCPUs).

Patch 2 is the important one: the TCB of threads created by
pthread_create never got multiple_threads set, so on x86 every
catomic_* operation ran without the lock prefix in all threads but the
main one.  A small multithreaded malloc/free test aborts within seconds
on 2 processors ("malloc_consolidate(): unaligned fastbin chunk
detected", "corrupted size vs. prev_size in fastbins"), and never on
one; multithreaded Hurd servers such as ext2fs corrupted their heaps.
The worker threads of that test read 0 at %fs:0x18; setting it makes
every run pass.

Patch 1 fixes a deadlock between a forking thread and the signal
thread, seen as a sporadic hang of /bin/sh during boot.

Testing: both were tested on Debian's glibc 2.41 (snapshot 20250807)
through binary patches: for patch 2 an equivalent one of
libpthread.so.0.3 (htl still lives there in that build), plus
ext2fs.static relinked with the same change; for patch 1 one that drops
the early resume_threads call, so that the threads are resumed by the
existing call after "lose:", i.e. somewhat later than this patch does,
but likewise after the signal thread state was fetched.  With them and the gnumach/hurd fixes
sent to bug-hurd, a parallel build/sort/tar/fork stress test passes 6
times out of 6 on 2 vCPUs.  I have not rebuilt glibc from these exact
sources.

This work was done together with Claude (an AI model by Anthropic),
hence the Co-Authored-By trailers.

Magnus Reimann (2):
  hurd: fork: fetch the signal thread state before resuming it
  hurd: Set multiple_threads in the TCB of new threads

